    "knowledge_point": "Calculating Intercepts of Spheres and Ellipsoids",
    "problem": "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{2}=1$, find its intercepts with the $z$-axis.",
    "worked_solution": "To find the $z$-intercepts, we plug $x=0$ and $y=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(0-3)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(0-1)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{7}{8}+\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ (z-2)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ z-2 & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z & =2\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $z=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $z=\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $z$-axis are $(0,0,\\frac{3}{2})$ and $(0,0,\\frac{5}{2}).$",
